# Vulnerabilities in npm's OWN bundled tooling (tar, brace-expansion,
# sigstore, glob, ip-address, minimatch, picomatch) — the copies npm vendors
# internally
# for its own package installation, dependency globbing, and provenance
# verification, not anything reachable via the running application.
# `next start` (this image's only runtime process, invoked through pnpm —
# see the Dockerfile's CMD) never shells out to npm or any of these; they
# only run during `npm install -g corepack@...` at BUILD time, to bootstrap
# a signature-verification-fixed Corepack (see the Dockerfile's own comment
# on nodejs/corepack#612).
#
# Confirmed via `pnpm why -r <pkg>` for each one: none are part of this
# project's own dependency tree at all — they trace entirely to npm 10.9.8,
# the newest version bundled with any published node:22-alpine image as of
# 2026-08-14 (node:22.23.2-alpine, this Dockerfile's pin). Verified directly
# against npm's own source (github.com/npm/cli @ v10.9.8's
# package-lock.json): it vendors tar@7.5.11, brace-expansion@2.0.2,
# sigstore@3.1.0, and older glob/ip-address/minimatch — each short of the
# fixed version below. This is upstream npm's own dependency bump to make,
# not something this project's lockfile or overrides can reach.
#
# (nanoid, postcss, and sharp were ALSO flagged in this same scan, but those
# ARE reachable through this project's own tree — next@15.5.23 depends on
# all three. Those were fixed for real via package.json's
# pnpm.overrides, not added here.)
#
# Re-check this list every time NODE_VERSION is bumped in
# infra/docker/Dockerfile — a newer bundled npm may already carry the fix,
# at which point the corresponding line(s) below should be deleted, not
# carried forward out of habit.

# tar — DoS via crafted gzip bomb (fixed 7.5.19; npm 10.9.8 vendors 7.5.11)
CVE-2026-59873
# tar — DoS via malformed tar archive header (fixed 7.5.18)
CVE-2026-59874
# tar — DoS via crafted long-path tar (fixed 7.5.21; same npm 10.9.8-vendored
# 7.5.11 as the two tar entries above — newly published 2026-08-22, not a
# new component or a new reachability question, the identical already-
# analyzed npm-bundled tar just getting a third disclosure)
CVE-2026-73566
# brace-expansion — DoS via exponential-time complexity (fixed 2.1.2 on the
# 2.x line; npm 10.9.8 vendors 2.0.2)
CVE-2026-13149
# brace-expansion — DoS via memory exhaustion in expand() (fixed 2.1.3)
CVE-2026-14257
# brace-expansion — DoS via unbounded intermediate arrays (fixed 2.1.4)
CVE-2026-69152
# sigstore — unauthorized certificates accepted due to ignored
# certificateOIDs verification option (fixed 4.1.1; npm 10.9.8 vendors 3.1.0)
CVE-2026-48815
# glob — command injection via malicious filenames (fixed 11.1.0/10.5.0)
CVE-2025-64756
# ip-address — inconsistent IP parsing leading to SSRF (fixed 10.3.1)
CVE-2026-69192
# minimatch — DoS via crafted glob patterns / catastrophic backtracking
# (fixed on every maintained line; npm 10.9.8's vendored copy predates all
# three)
CVE-2026-26996
CVE-2026-27903
CVE-2026-27904
# picomatch — ReDoS via crafted extglob patterns (fixed 4.0.4/3.0.2/2.3.2).
# Also confirmed via `pnpm why -r picomatch`: not in this project's tree.
CVE-2026-33671
